{
  "id": "functions-straight-line",
  "title": "Functions: The Straight Line",
  "term": 2,
  "summary": "The linear function $y = ax + q$: gradient and $y$-intercept, the effect of $a$ and $q$, intercepts, domain and range, and drawing and interpreting straight-line graphs.",
  "notes": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What is a function?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A **function** is a rule that gives exactly **one output** ($y$) for every input ($x$). Functions can be shown as a table, an equation, a graph or in words. We often write $f(x)$ instead of $y$: if $f(x) = 2x + 1$, then $f(3) = 2(3) + 1 = 7$."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "**Domain**: all possible input ($x$) values.",
        "**Range**: all possible output ($y$) values."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The linear function"
    },
    {
      "type": "formula",
      "latex": "y = ax + q",
      "caption": "$a$ is the gradient (slope) and $q$ is the $y$-intercept"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "caption": "The effect of $a$ and $q$",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Effect"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "$a > 0$",
          "The line slopes upwards from left to right (increasing)"
        ],
        [
          "$a < 0$",
          "The line slopes downwards from left to right (decreasing)"
        ],
        [
          "$a = 0$",
          "A horizontal line $y = q$"
        ],
        [
          "Larger $|a|$",
          "A steeper line"
        ],
        [
          "$q$",
          "Shifts the line up ($q > 0$) or down ($q < 0$); the line cuts the $y$-axis at $(0; q)$"
        ]
      ]
    },
